---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const bitsCfg = {
  params: [
    { name: 'bits', label: 'number of bits', min: 1, max: 16, step: 1, value: 8 },
  ],
  readouts: [
    { label: 'distinct values representable', expr: '2^bits' },
    { label: 'largest value storable', expr: '2^bits - 1' },
  ],
};

const binCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: 'weights: 8 4 2 1', note: 'A 4-bit number has place values 2³, 2², 2¹, 2⁰ — read right to left, exactly as decimal weights digits by 1000, 100, 10 and 1.' },
    { math: '13 ≥ 8 → digit 1, remainder 5', note: 'Turn the 8s-bit on; 13 − 8 = 5 remains.' },
    { math: '5 ≥ 4 → digit 1, remainder 1 · 2? → 0', note: 'Turn the 4s-bit on. Five cannot pay for a 2, so that digit stays 0.' },
    { math: '13 = 1101', note: 'Turn the 1s-bit on. Check: 8 + 4 + 0 + 1 = 13. Every number is just this — which is all a computer ever does.' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/computer-science/foundational/binary-bits">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    A transistor is a switch with two honest states: on and off. Computers are built from billions of
    them, so everything — numbers, photos, songs, this sentence — must be spelled in a two-letter
    alphabet. That is not a limitation philosophy chose; it is what physics offers cheaply. A voltage
    is never exactly 0 or exactly 5 volts — it is somewhere near, drifting with temperature and
    interference — and a system that only needs to decide "high or low?" can be wrong by a large margin
    and still be right. A system that tried to read ten distinct voltage levels per cell, decimal-style,
    would confuse 7 with 8 every time a wire got warm. <strong>Binary wins because reliability beats
    compactness</strong>: two states survive noise, ten do not. Everything in this lesson follows from
    that one engineering trade.
  </p>
  <p>
    One switch holds one <strong>bit</strong> — a single 0 or 1. Eight bits make a <strong>byte</strong>,
    which counts from 0 to 255 and is enough to store one typed letter ("A" is 65, or
    <code>01000001</code>). And the master pattern of the whole subject is arithmetic, not magic:
    <strong>n bits give 2ⁿ distinct values</strong>. Sixteen bits reach 65,536 (the "16-bit" era of
    games and sound cards); 24 bits of colour give 16,777,216 shades — the "true colour" of your screen;
    32 bits of memory address reach about 4 billion locations (a wall 2000s PCs actually hit); 64-bit
    processors address memory in spaces no machine has filled.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="why bytes stop at 255 — set the bit count and read the ceiling" widget="sliderLab" config={bitsCfg} />

  <p>
    Drag the slider and watch both readouts: the count of representable values and the largest storable
    number. They differ by exactly one, and the reason is that zero is a value too — with 8 bits you can
    name 256 different numbers, but one of them is 0, so the ceiling is 255, not 256. Every "out of
    memory", every "integer overflow", every year-2000-style bug is this slider failing quietly: the
    value you needed to store simply needed one more bit than the box had.
  </p>

  <h2>Number systems: binary, denary and hexadecimal</h2>
  <p>
    Binary is a place-value system exactly like decimal — only the base changes. Decimal 4,093 means
    4×1000 + 0×100 + 9×10 + 3×1; binary 1101 means 1×8 + 1×4 + 0×2 + 1×1 = 13. Converting
    denary → binary is the widget below's greedy algorithm: repeatedly subtract the largest power of two
    that fits. Converting binary → denary is even easier: add the place values under every 1. Two
    refinements complete the number-system toolkit. <strong>Hexadecimal</strong> (base 16, digits
    0–9 then A = 10 through F = 15) is not a third counting system the hardware uses — the machine only
    ever sees binary — but a human shorthand, because one hex digit encodes exactly four bits (one
    <em>nybble</em>): 1111 = F, 1010 0111 = A7. Memory addresses, colour codes (#FF8000 = red 255, green
    128, blue 0) and error dumps are written in hex because a 32-bit address in binary is 32 characters
    and in hex is 8. <strong>Negative numbers</strong> use <strong>two's complement</strong>: flip every
    bit and add 1, so −13 in 8 bits is 11110011. The beautiful consequence is that the same adder
    circuit handles subtraction for free (a − b = a + (−b)), with no separate subtraction hardware —
    and the leading bit doubles as a sign flag: 0 means non-negative, 1 means negative. The cost is a
    fixed range (−128 to +127 for 8 bits) and the famous <strong>overflow</strong> error when a result
    exceeds the bits available — 127 + 1 in 8 bits wraps around to −128, which is the arithmetic
    version of the odometer rolling over.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="counting like a computer — 13 in binary, honestly" widget="equationStep" config={binCfg} />

  <h2>Text: agreeing on lookup tables</h2>
  <p>
    Text is the easiest encoding to understand because it is pure agreement. <strong>ASCII</strong>
    (1963) assigned numbers to characters: 65 = A, 97 = a, 48 = 0, 32 = space — originally 7 bits, 128
    characters, enough for English and arithmetic and nothing else (no é, no 中文, no emoji). The
    successor, <strong>Unicode</strong>, assigns a number to <em>every</em> character in every living
    writing system — over 150,000 of them — and defines encodings (UTF-8 the most common) that store
    those numbers as one to four bytes each, chosen so that plain English text still costs one byte per
    letter and stays backward-compatible with ASCII. The deep point is that a character set is a
    dictionary between symbols and integers; once text is integers, it is bits, and every operation —
    sorting, searching, upper-casing — becomes arithmetic on codes. It also means encoding mismatches
    are real bugs: reading a UTF-8 file as an older single-byte encoding is exactly how you get
    "cafÃ©" instead of "café" — the bytes are the same, the dictionary used to decode them differs.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> letters are not "stored" in memory. Only integers are stored;
  letters exist at the moment of interpretation — 65 is the code for A when the program treats it as
  text, and a perfectly good number 65 when it doesn't. The same bytes can be a character, a pixel
  value or an instruction depending entirely on what the reader expects.</p>

  <h2>Images and sound: sampling reality</h2>
  <p>
    Both image and audio digitisation are the same idea — <em>measure at intervals, store the
    measurements as numbers</em> — applied in two dimensions of different kinds. A <strong>bitmap
    image</strong> is a grid of <strong>pixels</strong>; each pixel's colour is a number whose size is
    the <strong>colour depth</strong> (24-bit true colour = 8 bits each for red, green and blue). Two
    dials control quality: <strong>resolution</strong> (how many pixels) and colour depth (how many
    colours each can be). File size follows immediately: width × height × depth ÷ 8 bytes. A 1920 ×
    1080 photo at 24 bits is 1920 × 1080 × 3 ≈ 6.2 MB before compression. Digital
    <strong>sound</strong> measures a wave's amplitude over time: the <strong>sampling rate</strong> is
    how many measurements per second (CD quality: 44,100 Hz — chosen because human hearing ends near
    20 kHz and you must sample at better than twice the highest frequency you want to keep), and the
    <strong>sample resolution</strong> is how many bits each measurement gets (16-bit for CD: 65,536
    possible loudness levels). File size = rate × resolution × channels × seconds; one stereo minute at
    CD quality is 44,100 × 16 × 2 × 60 ≈ 10.6 MB. The two dials trade fidelity against size in both
    media, and increasing either multiplies file size linearly — which is why the third topic, compression,
    is not optional but structural.
  </p>

  <h2>Storage units and compression</h2>
  <p>
    First the units, because the gap here confuses everyone: <strong>KiB vs KB</strong>. Memory is
    binary — 1 KiB = 1,024 bytes = 2¹⁰ — while storage marketing uses decimal — 1 KB = 1,000 bytes.
    The gap compounds: a "1 TB" drive holds 10¹² bytes, which is about 931 GiB, and that is why your
    drive shows ~931 GB. Nothing is missing; two definitions of "giga" are meeting. (The ISO/IEC 80000-13
    standard created the "i" units — kibibyte, mebibyte, gibibyte — precisely to stop the argument.)
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Compression</strong> exploits redundancy: real data repeats itself, and repetition can be
    described more briefly than it occurs. <strong>Lossless</strong> compression shrinks a file and can
    restore it bit-for-bit — essential for text, code and archives. <strong>Run-length encoding</strong>
    stores runs as (count, value) pairs: the row WWWWWWWWWWWWBWWWW becomes 12W1B4W; great for cartoons
    with flat colour, useless for photographs where runs are short. Dictionary methods (LZ77 and
    descendants, inside ZIP and PNG) replace repeated phrases with references to earlier occurrences.
    <strong>Lossy</strong> compression throws information away — <em>permanently</em> — but throws away
    the information humans barely perceive. JPEG discards colour detail the eye is less sensitive to
    than brightness and keeps only a subset of spatial frequencies; MP3 discards frequencies and
    "masked" sounds that psychoacoustic research says you will not miss. The compression ratios are
    dramatic (10:1 is normal) and the loss is, by design, invisible — until you re-save a JPEG a
    hundred times and watch artifacts accumulate, because each pass loses a little more. The rule that
    tells you which to use: if the data's <em>meaning</em> must survive exactly (text, medical records,
    programs), lossless only; if the data's <em>appearance</em> is the point (photos, music, video),
    lossy is usually the honest bargain.
  </p>

  <h2>Binary arithmetic: addition, shifts, and hex in practice</h2>
  <p>
    Binary arithmetic works column-by-column exactly like the arithmetic you know, with simpler
    carries. Add 0110 (6) + 0111 (7): rightmost 0+1 = 1; next 1+1 = 0 carry 1; next 1+1+1 = 1 carry 1;
    leftmost 0+0+1 = 1 — result 1101 (13). Each column's rule is tiny (0+0 = 0, 0+1 = 1, 1+1 = 0
    carry 1, 1+1+1 = 1 carry 1), and that simplicity is the point: the same adder circuit, replicated
    and chained, adds numbers of any width. Two more operations show why binary suits machinery.
    <strong>Shifting left</strong> (every bit moves one place up, 0 fills the gap) multiplies by two —
    0110 (6) shifted left is 1100 (12) — exactly as appending a zero multiplies by ten in decimal;
    <strong>shifting right</strong> divides by two, discarding the remainder. Multiplication by a
    constant becomes add-and-shift: ×10 is (×8) + (×2), two shifts and one add. And a worked hex
    conversion closes the loop: split 10101101 into nybbles 1010 | 1101, look each up (1010 = 10 = A,
    1101 = 13 = D), read AD — the whole reason memory dumps and colour codes are written in hex is
    that this two-step translation is error-proof where reading thirty-two raw bits is not.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Why is there a limit on compression? Because information has a measurable quantity:
    <strong>entropy</strong>, defined by Claude Shannon in 1948 as (roughly) the base-2 logarithm of the
    number of possible messages. A source with 8 equally likely symbols carries 3 bits per symbol, and
    no lossless scheme can beat that average — compression can only remove the gap between a symbol's
    raw size and its true probability. That is why a text file zips well (letters are wildly uneven in
    frequency) and a file of random bytes zips not at all (every value equally likely — no redundancy
    <em>is</em> maximum entropy). Shannon's coding theorems set the exact boundary, and practical coders
    approach it: Huffman coding assigns short bit patterns to frequent symbols and long patterns to
    rare ones; arithmetic coding squeezes closer to the entropy limit. The other deep current is the
    trade between number systems and hardware: binary-coded decimal survives in financial processors
    precisely because decimal fractions like 0.1 have no exact binary representation (0.1 in binary is
    an infinitely repeating pattern, the reason 0.1 + 0.2 ≠ 0.3 in floating point), and banks would
    rather pay in hardware than let a penny drift. And the frontier is still place-value: quantum
    computers replace the bit with the qubit, which can hold a <em>superposition</em> of 0 and 1 — but
    every classical machine you will ever touch remains, at bottom, a very fast arrangement of
    two-letter spellings of the world. Learn to read 1101 and you have learned the alphabet.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Shannon, "A Mathematical Theory of Communication" (1948); Petzold, Code: The Hidden Language of Computer Hardware and Software; ISO/IEC 80000-13 units standard; Cambridge International AS & A Level Computer Science 9618 syllabus (data representation).</p>
</TopicPage>
